// [Characters Build Languages.note]    search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is not an attempt to internationalize the text input and output beyond the Ascii standards.
   The Ascii standards and World Wide Web Internet eliminated my first real job out of college.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   I was assigned to be part of a team reconciling the character size (N # of 0/1 bits) for values
   traveling through a private network between mainframe computers.  The computers varied in their
   design and architecture differences at a very deep level for both processors and networks.  The
   networks were integrated from the card punch machine (80 characters per card) all the way to the
   machine code software loading program writing the machine code into memory on the mainframe
   that caused the computer to signal the printer (on paper 80 characters wide) through wires that
   a page feed should occur now.  The wire from the mainframe to the printer powered something on,
   such as a motor connected to the gears holding the paper, long enough to move the paper.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Processors and mainframes were developed by IBM, DEC, XEROX; this was pre-x86, 68K, ARM micros.
   This was at a company selling access to a variety of large computers with networks between them;
   all similar computers sharing a network design. The service of moving data between dissimilar
   computers came from customers integrating applications more geographically, because they could.
   -------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------
   How did a mature 1979 computer industry compete with their technological solutions? Lower costs.
   A 7-bit number system sufficient for an application removed the cost for 1 wire everywhere in a
   large computer built from tubes and wires; at the time the integrated circuit was just emerging.
   Operations were implemented by electronic circuits designed to be as fast and small as possible.
   Moving from 7-bit to 9-bit made the math range bigger throughout the system, which people wanted.
   The introduction of the IC made it easy to create multiples; the choice was multiples of 8 bits.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The networking byte-size issue and the meaning of each value were defined by Ascii standards.
   The order of bytes within a multi-byte value, e.g., a 16-bit integer, exposed each host's choice
   about byte-order-for-an-N-bit-number.  The transition to an 8-bit common view was probably done
   as soon as companies like mine addressed the problem.  The transition to a byte-order for a
   32-bit number remains a function of basic processor design, and must be considered by devices
   autonomously accessing memory that is within the memory map of a processor and processor bus.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The primary signal carrier was a large magnetic tape or a very small magnetic disk drive.  Even
   on a magnetic tape with zeros and ones written in a pattern, the order of the bits was a choice.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Definition of any the characters used for input matching or output descriptions are named here
   in the interest of platform/mesa independence and portability to other languages than American.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef     Bits8_t         Ascii_t;           a value container whose size is sizeof (Bits8_t)

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Elevating the Ascii character abstraction now to include "a collection of Ascii characters".
   for normal code to emit a quoted (e.g, "text") series of Ascii characters without upsetting HAL.
   Null is used by pointer code to refer to "not a valid pointer to an array of Ascii values", with
   pointer shorthand for tables: e.g., pAsciiANull, pAsciiANull, pAsciiANull can be pAAN, pAAN, pAAN.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define     S(inquotesAsciiArray)   ((pAsciiA_t) inquotesAsciiArray)

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Ascii characters can be emitted using a pointer (_pAa) or ssHL-language-language quoted "string" (_qAa).
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   void        mesa_uiOp_emit_pAsciiA              (pAsciiA_t pAsciiA);

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
